//! lotus/ring_mod.rye — ALES75: the ring modulator — the tremolo's carrier let CROSS ZERO. ALES74's tremolo pointed the triangle LFO at the
//! sample's amplitude and held the result to an ATTENUATION — a gain between silence and unity, so the voice was never louder than the dry and
//! the write's saturate was a proven no-op. The ring modulator is that rung with ONE RESTRICTION LIFTED: the carrier is allowed to cross zero
//! and go negative. A carrier that dips below zero flips the sample's sign; swung fast it multiplies the signal by a bipolar wave and scatters
//! the spectrum into metallic sum-and-difference tones — the ring modulator's unmistakable clang. It is the tremolo's named sibling.
//!
//! The crux is that A RING MODULATOR WHOSE CARRIER NEVER GOES NEGATIVE IS EXACTLY THE TREMOLO — the two rungs are one multiply, the carrier's
//! sign the only difference. The write is the tremolo's verbatim: y[i] = saturate(x[i]·c(i)/glide_scale), c the reused ALES71 triangle. The one
//! change is the carrier's legal range: where the tremolo required 0 ≤ c ≤ glide_scale (an attenuation), the ring modulator requires only
//! −glide_scale ≤ c ≤ glide_scale (a unit-bounded BIPOLAR carrier). The magnitude bound at unity stays, so the voice is still never louder than
//! the dry — only its sign may turn.
//!
//! There is one honest subtlety the tremolo never met: the two's-complement i16 rail is ASYMMETRIC (sample_min = −32768, sample_max = 32767),
//! so inverting sample_min overflows by exactly one — −(−32768) = 32768, one past sample_max. The tremolo's non-negative gain could never
//! invert a sample, so its saturate was a no-op; the ring modulator's carrier at −unity on a full-negative sample lands 32768, and the one true
//! saturate GENUINELY EARNS ITS KEEP, clamping that lone corner to 32767 rather than wrapping. So x·c/scale sits in [−32768, 32768], and the
//! write saturates the single overflowing point honestly — the ring modulator's real distinction from the tremolo, not a restatement of it.
//! Every output depends only on its own input sample, so the ring modulator writes IN PLACE WITH NO SNAPSHOT (the tremolo's thinnest discipline).
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES75.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-201937_fill-ales75-lotus-ring-modulator-bipolar-carrier.md
//! Stands on: lotus/tremolo.rye (ALES74) — the amplitude write this generalizes by removing the floor; lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71) — the
//! triangle-LFO driver `triangle_delay_at`, now the carrier; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) — `glide_scale`, the fixed-point unit (256 = unity);
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/max, the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM in one clip, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the carrier is counted in scaled units (256 = unity, −256 = inverted unity) and the period in
//! samples, not hertz. No delay line, no snapshot, no lookahead. The triangle and the multiply run in i64 and the write saturates once — a
//! documented no-op EXCEPT the lone sample_min-inverted corner, where it clamps 32768 to 32767 rather than wrapping.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/ring_mod.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const chorus = @import("chorus.rye");
const tremolo = @import("tremolo.rye");

pub const ring_mod_version = "20260814.201937";

/// The ring modulator's faults, one consistent name each: a period that is not a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors
/// need P/4 whole) or wider than max_clip, a negative depth (a swing has a magnitude, never a sign), a carrier whose swing passes unity in
/// EITHER direction (|centre| + depth past glide_scale — past that the voice could exceed the rail by more than the lone two's-complement
/// corner), and a span outside the samples. Each refused before any write. There is NO BadGain (the one-sided tremolo bound widens to the
/// two-sided BadCarrier), NO BadDelay, and NO BadLevel — the carrier IS the level.
pub const RingModError = error{
    /// A period that is not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip — refused, no write.
    BadPeriod,
    /// A negative depth — a swing has a magnitude, never a sign — refused, no write.
    BadDepth,
    /// A carrier whose swing passes unity in either direction (centre + depth > glide_scale, or centre − depth < −glide_scale) — refused, no write.
    BadCarrier,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Apply a ring modulator over `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: a triangle carrier of `depth_scaled` round
/// `centre_scaled` over `period` samples (ALES71's `triangle_delay_at`, read as a BIPOLAR carrier in units of glide_scale) multiplies each
/// sample — y[i] = saturate(x[i]·c(i)/glide_scale). No delay line and no snapshot: every output depends only on its own input sample. The
/// carrier is unit-bounded (|c| ≤ glide_scale), so the voice is never louder than the dry; the write saturates once — a no-op except the lone
/// sample_min-inverted corner (−32768·−1 = 32768 clamped to 32767). A non-negative carrier makes this EXACTLY the tremolo. Refuses BadPeriod,
/// BadDepth, BadCarrier, and BadRange before any write; on refusal the clip is untouched. Values only — never a length.
/// Validate a ring modulator's period, carrier swing, and span together, before any sample is touched — the four faults refused up front so a
/// caller (the mono `ring_mod`, or a stereo lift running the same carrier on each channel) can prove atomicity once against the shared length.
/// The period must be a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need P/4 whole) and no wider than max_clip; the depth
/// non-negative; the carrier's swing within ±unity in BOTH directions (|centre| + depth ≤ glide_scale — the two-sided magnitude bound that keeps
/// |y| ≤ |x|); and the span wholly within the samples. Refuses BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadCarrier, and BadRange.
pub fn precheck(clip_len: u32, start: u32, count: u32, centre_scaled: i64, depth_scaled: i64, period: u32) RingModError!void {
    // invariant: the period is a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need P/4 whole) and no wider than the widest
    // clip, so the carrier is bounded. Checked at the edge before any read.
    if (period == 0 or period % 4 != 0) return error.BadPeriod;
    if (period > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadPeriod;
    // invariant: the depth is non-negative — a swing has a magnitude, never a sign.
    if (depth_scaled < 0) return error.BadDepth;
    // invariant: the carrier's swing stays within ±unity in BOTH directions — the tremolo's one-sided attenuation bound widened to a
    // two-sided magnitude bound. This is what keeps |y| ≤ |x| (so the only rail-touching point is the lone sample_min inversion).
    if (centre_scaled + depth_scaled > glide.glide_scale) return error.BadCarrier;
    if (centre_scaled - depth_scaled < -glide.glide_scale) return error.BadCarrier;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip_len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip_len - start) return error.BadRange;
}

pub fn ring_mod(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
) RingModError!void {
    // invariant: the period, carrier swing, and span are all legal — the four faults refused before any read, so a refusal never half-writes.
    try precheck(clip.len, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);

    const scale: i64 = glide.glide_scale;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        // ALES71's `triangle_delay_at` is a general scaled triangle — here it is the BIPOLAR CARRIER. c in [centre − depth, centre + depth].
        const c: i64 = chorus.triangle_delay_at(centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, k);
        // invariant: the carrier never leaves the validated unit-magnitude window, so |c| stays at or below unity.
        assert(c >= -scale and c <= scale);
        const dry: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]);
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and dry <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // The ring-modulated voice: dry times the carrier. |dry| ≤ 32768 and |c| ≤ scale, so the product fits i64 and the quotient's
        // magnitude is no larger than |dry| ≤ 32768. The ONLY value outside the i16 rail is +32768 (dry = sample_min, c = −scale).
        const rung: i64 = @divTrunc(dry * c, scale);
        // invariant: the voice sits in [sample_min, sample_max + 1] — in the rail except the lone sample_min-inverted corner (+32768), which
        // the saturate below clamps to sample_max. This is where the ring modulator's saturate genuinely earns its keep, unlike the tremolo's.
        assert(rung >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and rung <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max) + 1);
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(rung);
    }
    // invariant: a ring modulator changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: A NON-NEGATIVE CARRIER IS THE TREMOLO (the family identity, proven against the real tremolo); a hand-computed bipolar case
//     shows the sign flip; a constant −unity carrier inverts; unity passthrough is exact; the lone two's-complement corner saturates rather
//     than wraps; and each fault refuses by name, the clip untouched ----------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;

    // 1. A NON-NEGATIVE CARRIER IS THE TREMOLO — the family identity. With centre − depth ≥ 0 the carrier never goes negative, so the ring
    //    modulator equals the tremolo at every sample (both run). A modulation wide enough to exercise the swing, on a loud signal (including
    //    sample_min, where a non-negative gain still never overflows — the inversion corner belongs to the bipolar case alone).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, -6000, 3000, -4000, 30000, -30000, 7000, -8000 };
        const centre: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);
        const depth: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2); // centre − depth = 0, carrier stays in [0, scale]
        var ring = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&ring, &sig);
        try ring_mod(&ring, 0, ring.len, centre, depth, 8);
        var trem = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&trem, &sig);
        try tremolo.tremolo(&trem, 0, trem.len, centre, depth, 8);
        if (!eq(ring.samples(), trem.samples())) return error.NonNegNotTremolo;
    }

    // 2. A HAND-COMPUTED BIPOLAR CASE — centre 0, depth scale, period 4 (q = 1): the carrier traces the four anchors 0 → +1 → 0 → −1 → 0.
    //      k:      0   1     2   3     4   5
    //      c(k):   0   256   0   -256  0   256   (0, +1, 0, −1, 0, +1 of scale = 256)
    //      y[k]: 1000·c/256 =  0  1000  0  -1000  0  1000    — the sign flip made visible.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000, 1000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, scale, 4);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, 1000, 0, -1000, 0, 1000 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.BipolarHandWrong;
    }

    // 3. A CONSTANT CARRIER AT −UNITY INVERTS THE SIGNAL — centre −scale, depth 0: y = x·(−scale)/scale = −x exactly, on a signal with no
    //    sample_min (where inversion is exact and overflows nothing).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -200, 300, -400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, -scale, 0, 4);
        const want = [_]i16{ -100, 200, -300, 400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.NegUnityNotInvert;
    }

    // 4. UNITY PASSTHROUGH IS EXACT — centre = scale, depth = 0: the carrier is +1, so every sample passes through byte-for-byte, including
    //    sample_max at the top of the rail.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_max, -1, 0, 12345, -12345 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, scale, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.UnityNotPassthrough;
    }

    // 5. THE LONE TWO'S-COMPLEMENT CORNER SATURATES, NEVER WRAPS — sample_min under a −unity carrier: −(−32768) = 32768, one past sample_max,
    //    clamped to sample_max rather than wrapping to −32768. This is the one place the ring modulator's saturate does real work.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, timeline.sample_min });
        try ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, -scale, 0, 4);
        const want = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_max, timeline.sample_max };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.CornerWrapped;
    }

    // 6a. A PERIOD not a positive multiple of four refuses BadPeriod; zero refuses BadPeriod; a period wider than max_clip refuses BadPeriod —
    //     before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, scale, 6)) |_| return error.OddPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongOddPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedOddPeriodMutated;
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, scale, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongZeroPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedZeroPeriodMutated;
        const wide: u32 = timeline.max_clip + 4;
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, scale, wide)) |_| return error.WidePeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongWidePeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedWidePeriodMutated;
    }

    // 6b. A NEGATIVE DEPTH refuses BadDepth; a carrier swing past +unity refuses BadCarrier; a carrier swing past −unity refuses BadCarrier —
    //     each before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, -1, 4)) |_| return error.NegDepthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongNegDepth;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedNegDepthMutated;
        // centre ½·scale, depth ¾·scale → top = centre + depth > glide_scale
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, @divTrunc(scale, 2), @divTrunc(3 * scale, 4), 4)) |_| return error.HighTopSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCarrier) return error.WrongHighTop;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHighTopMutated;
        // centre −½·scale, depth ¾·scale → bottom = centre − depth < −glide_scale
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 0, clip.len, -@divTrunc(scale, 2), @divTrunc(3 * scale, 4), 4)) |_| return error.LowBotSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCarrier) return error.WrongLowBot;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedLowBotMutated;
    }

    // 6c. AN OUT-OF-RANGE SPAN refuses BadRange before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (ring_mod(&clip, 2, 5, 0, scale, 4)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-ring-mod: the RING MODULATOR — the tremolo's triangle carrier let CROSS ZERO. It writes the tremolo's line verbatim, " ++
        "y[i] = saturate(x[i]·c(i)/glide_scale), and only widens the carrier's legal range from the tremolo's one-sided attenuation " ++
        "(0 ≤ c ≤ scale) to a two-sided unit-bounded BIPOLAR carrier (−scale ≤ c ≤ scale); a carrier below zero flips the sample's sign, the " ++
        "metallic clang. So A NON-NEGATIVE CARRIER IS EXACTLY THE TREMOLO, proven sample-by-sample against the real tremolo; a hand-computed " ++
        "bipolar case (0 → +1 → 0 → −1) shows the sign flip; a constant −unity carrier inverts; unity passthrough is exact; and the lone " ++
        "two's-complement corner — sample_min under a −unity carrier, −(−32768) = 32768 — SATURATES to sample_max rather than wrapping, the " ++
        "one place the ring modulator's saturate does real work the tremolo's never did. A period not a positive multiple of four refuses " ++
        "BadPeriod, a negative depth BadDepth, a carrier past unity in either direction BadCarrier, an out-of-range span BadRange, each " ++
        "leaving the clip untouched — there is no BadGain, no BadDelay, and no BadLevel, the carrier IS the level; no delay line, no snapshot, " ++
        "no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. Auto-pan (the same LFO on the stereo field) is the " ++
        "family's next rung\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
